## Security decisions summary

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| Sanitize SVG server-side with DOMPurify + explicit allowlist | SVGs are active content; never trust client input |
| Serve with `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox` | Defense-in-depth — even a sanitization bypass can't execute scripts |
| Server-generated UUID filenames | Prevents path traversal and filename collisions |
| Rate limit per authenticated user | Prevents abuse of upload endpoint |
| Path resolution check before file read | Blocks path traversal attacks |
| Return 404 for missing/unauthorized files | Prevents resource enumeration |
| No `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` or raw SVG rendering client-side | SVG is served as an `<img>` src, which browsers treat as inert |

## Client usage (safe pattern)

```tsx
// src/components/Avatar.tsx
// Security: Render avatar via <img> tag — browsers treat SVG in <img> as
// inert (no script execution). Never use dangerouslySetInnerHTML for SVG.
export function Avatar({ url, alt }: { url: string; alt: string }) {
  return <img src={url} alt={alt} width={48} height={48} loading="lazy" />;
}
